ects.com/images/search.svg) ![](https://www.energyconnects.com/images/search-close.svg)\n\n- [Home](https://www.energyconnects.com/)\n- [News](https://www.energyconnects.com/news/)\n- [Oil](https://www.energyconnects.com/news/oil/)\n- Oil Steadies as NYT Reports Trump Is Weighing Fresh Iran Strikes\n\n[News](https://www.energyconnects.com/news/) [Oil](https://www.energyconnects.com/news/oil/)\n\n# Oil Steadies as NYT Reports Trump Is Weighing Fresh Iran Strikes\n\nBy Bloomberg\n\nNov 19, 2026\n\n![BloombergMedia_TOON56KJH6V500_19-11-2026_05-00-03_639306432000000000.jpg](https://www.energyconnects.com/media/j4wl2zpr/bloombergmedia_toon56kjh6v500_19-11-2026_05-00-03_639306432000000000.jpg?width=1050&amp;v=1dd77e2d68f9070&amp;format=webp &quot;BloombergMedia_TOON56KJH6V500_19-11-2026_05-00-03_639306432000000000.jpg&quot;)\n\nBloomberg\n\n[![Add Energy Connects as a preferred source on Google](https://www.energyconnects.com/images/google_badge/google_preferred_source_badge_light.png)](https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=energyconnects.com)\n\n(Bloomberg) -- Oil steadied as traders assessed the outlook for the US-Iran conflict, including the possibility of fresh attacks against Tehran. \n\nBrent held near $92 a barrel after falling on Wednesday, while West Texas Intermediate was around $83. President Donald Trump is weighing plans for further strikes, the New York Times reported, citing US and Israeli officials. Trump has yet to make a decision, but Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu is pressing him to join a four- to seven-day campaign, it said.\n\n![](https://assets.bwbx.io/images/users/iqjWHBFdfxIU/iHlSm39lNIaY/v3/-1x-1.png?format=webp)\n\nGlobal benchmark Brent has retreated by more than a fifth so far this month, as supplies from the Middle East recovered and Trump held off on renewed large-scale strikes against the Islamic Republic. Still, there’s been a flare-up in low-level fighting across the region in recent days, including a spate of attacks on vessels transiting the critical Strait of Hormuz.\n\n“We are not yet at the point where the narrative of improving supply is enough to overshadow the military flare-ups in the region,” said Florence Schmit, a senior energy strategist at Rabobank. “Flows through Hormuz have improved, yet ships are still being attacked and President Trump is yet again considering plans for another round of extensive strikes on Iran.”\n\nTehran reiterated to neighboring countries they should bar US attacks from their soil, according to the Foreign Ministry. Failing to do so was “an act of aggression,” and entitled Iran to take “defensive measures,” it said.\n\nMeanwhile, one of three US carrier strike groups enforcing the blockade on Iran has left the Persian Gulf and is likely returning to Japan, Bloomberg reported. That came after the Theodore Roosevelt group arrived Monday.\n\nAway from the Middle East, traders will track the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit in Shenzhen, where Trump is set to meet later Thursday with his Chinese and Russian counterparts, Xi Jinping and Vladimir Putin. Trump told Fox News he was not going to ask them for help with the Iran war.\n\nOn the demand front, China’s appetite is rebounding faster than expected as lower prices spur refiners to use import quotas and rebuild stockpiles. Energy Aspects Ltd., FGE NexantECA and Rystad Energy AS have raised fourth-quarter import forecasts, ranging from 9.8 million to 10.2 million barrels a day. \n\nOil fell on Wednesday as official US data showed crude inventories increased 3.4 million barrels last week, while product stockpiles also expanded. In addition, there was a broad retreat across commodities, while the US dollar rose, presenting a headwind for raw materials priced in the currency.\n\nIn the US, nearly all Federal Reserve officials agreed in October further rate hikes would likely be needed, with two signaling a move of more than 25 basis points could be warranted in December if inflation remained elevated, according to minutes of their last meeting released Wednesday. \n\n“A stronger dollar and more hawkish rhetoric from Fed officials have weighed heavily on the commodities complex, with oil unable to escape this pressure,” said Warren Patterson, head of commodities strategy for ING Groep NV. “While improving fundamentals justify the pullback in prices, there are still clear supply risks hanging over the market when it comes to the Persian Gulf.” \n\n©2026 Bloomberg L.P.\n\nBy Kanoko Matsuyama , Lorelei Smillie\n\n## You might also like\n\n- [![BloombergMedia_TOMW5JT9NJLS00_18-11-2026_12-19-52_639305568000000000.jpg](https://www.energyconnects.com/media/nk1ptj2q/bloombergmedia_tomw5jt9njls00_18-11-2026_12-19-52_639305568000000000.jpg?width=50&amp;height=28&amp;quality=40&amp;v=1dd77571d297820 &quot;Oil Edges Lower Despite Gulf Flare-Ups as Traders Shun Risk&quot;) Oil Oil Edges Lower Despite Gulf Flare-Ups as Traders Shun Risk](https://www.energyconnects.com/news/oil/2026/november/oil-edges-lower-despite-gulf-flare-ups-as-traders-shun-risk/)\n- [![BloombergMedia_TOKY3QT96OSG00_17-11-2026_05-27-36_639304704000000000.jpg](https://www.energyconnects.com/media/yhppwdis/bloombergmedia_toky3qt96osg00_17-11-2026_05-27-36_639304704000000000.jpg?width=50&amp;height=28&amp;quality=40&amp;v=1dd76545b227c20 &quot;Oil Drops as Supplies Recover and Vance Offers Caution on Iran&quot;) Oil Oil Drops as Supplies Recover and Vance Offers Caution on Iran](https://www.energyconnects.com/news/oil/2026/november/oil-drops-as-supplies-recover-and-vance-offers-caution-on-iran/)\n\n## KEEPING THE ENERGY INDUSTRY CONNECTED\n\n### Subscribe to our newsletter and get the best of Energy Connects directly to your inbox each week.\n\nBy subscribing, you agree to the processing of your personal data by dmg events as described in the [Privacy Policy.](https://www.energyconnects.com/subscribe/ &quot;Subscribe&quot;)\n\n[JOIN THE CONVERSATION](https://www.energyconnects.com/subscribe/)\n\